# Target C-II — interior-block averaging gives a much larger synchronized train/diameter rectangle

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** coupled O--O path; complete-train interval-neighborhood/right-endpoint argument; train ledger; phase-free path-region saturation; common diameter hub and one-sided switching; metric grid span; post-synchronization phase purification  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_interior_block_sync_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The existing complete-rectangle proof partitions the selected O--O path into nine blocks and uses one block with few crossing-interval right endpoints. The later diameter synchronization then discards a hub side and another median half of that block. Those fixed losses are avoidable.

The key observation is that only three path blocks need be forbidden before the right-endpoint averaging:

1. the first block, so a chosen pre-hub block has a full outward block of regions before it;
2. the last block, for the symmetric post-hub case; and
3. at most one block that actually straddles the common hub region.

Every other block is already wholly on one hub side and has a full neighboring block in the outward direction. Averaging the global `3.0605D` right-endpoint budget over those eligible blocks produces a complete train rectangle that is synchronized with an equally large family of actual diameter supports **without any subsequent side/median factor**.

For an `N`-block partition, `N>=8`, define

\[
a_N:={0.0002038\over N},
\qquad
b_N:=0.6536-{3.0605\over N-3}.
\tag{IB.1}
\]

Then `b_N>0`, and the high-`t` survivor contains families

\[
E_N,\quad A_N,\quad B_N
\]

with

\[
|E_N|,|A_N|>a_ND-o(D),
\qquad
|B_N|>b_ND-o(D),
\tag{IB.2}
\]

such that

\[
E_N\times A_N
\]

is complete in exact selected-cut support incidence and

\[
A_N\times B_N
\]

is complete in strict-rhombus incidence, with both train shores internally noncrossing.

Two parameter choices are useful.

- `N=8` maximizes the guaranteed synchronized diameter/row density among the positive family:
  \[
  |E_8|,|A_8|>2.5475\times10^{-5}D-o(D),
  \qquad
  |B_8|>0.0415D-o(D).
  \]
- `N=14` maximizes the guaranteed raw train-grid product `a_N b_N`:
  \[
  |E_{14}|,|A_{14}|>1.45571428\times10^{-5}D-o(D),
  \]
  \[
  |B_{14}|>0.3753727D-o(D).
  \]

After choosing one consistent transverse orientation and applying the post-synchronization phase-purification theorem with `epsilon=0.1`, the `N=14` scaffold contains

\[
|E_{14}|>1.45571428\times10^{-5}D-o(D),
\]

\[
|A_{14,90}|>1.31014285\times10^{-5}D-o(D),
\]

\[
|B_{14,90}|>0.1689177D-o(D),
\]

with every train-grid angle equal to `pi/3+O(1/D)`, explicit defect coefficient below `4.09e7/D`, row-normal metric span above `0.14628D-o(D)`, and column-normal metric span above `1.1346e-5D-o(D)`.

This strictly improves the previous post-synchronization phase scaffold in both train-shore cardinalities while more than doubling the synchronized actual-diameter population.

---

## 1. An `N`-block version of the complete-rectangle argument

Write the selected O--O path as

\[
\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m,
\]

with

\[
\boxed{m>\rho D-o(D),
\qquad \rho=0.0002038.}
\tag{IB.3}
\]

Every selected row satisfies the established crossing-neighbor floor

\[
\boxed{|\Gamma(\tau_j)|>rD-o(D),
\qquad r=0.6536.}
\tag{IB.4}
\]

For every complete stopped train `sigma`, its selected-path crossing set

\[
I_\sigma:=\{j:\sigma\text{ crosses }\tau_j\}
\]

is an interval. The total number of nonempty interval right endpoints, counted with train multiplicity, is at most the complete-train count

\[
\boxed{T_{tr}<TD+o(D),
\qquad T=3.0605.}
\tag{IB.5}
\]

Fix an integer `N>=8` and partition the selected path into `N` consecutive blocks of sizes differing by at most one. Every block has

\[
>{\rho\over N}D-o(D)=a_ND-o(D)
\tag{IB.6}
\]

rows.

Let the common diameter-routing hub be the complementary region `R_k`. At most one path block can contain selected rows on both sides of `R_k`: such a block must contain the adjacent pair `tau_k,tau_{k+1}`. Call it the hub-straddling block if it exists.

Exclude:

- the first path block;
- the last path block;
- the hub-straddling block, if any.

There remain at least `N-3` **eligible** blocks. Each eligible block is wholly on one strict side of the hub and, because it is not a path-end block, has a full neighboring path block in the direction away from the hub.

Assign every nonempty interval `I_sigma` to the block containing its right endpoint. By (IB.5), some eligible block `J` receives at most

\[
{T\over N-3}D+o(D)
\tag{IB.7}
\]

right endpoints.

Let `tau_a` be the first selected row in `J`. A complete train that crosses `tau_a` but fails to cross every row of `J` must have its right endpoint inside `J`, exactly as in the original nine-block proof. Therefore

\[
\left|\bigcap_{\tau_j\in J}\Gamma(\tau_j)\right|
>
\left(r-{T\over N-3}\right)D-o(D).
\]

Set

\[
A_N:=J,
\qquad
B_N:=\bigcap_{\tau_j\in J}\Gamma(\tau_j).
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{|A_N|>a_ND-o(D),}
\tag{IB.8}
\]

\[
\boxed{|B_N|>b_ND-o(D),}
\tag{IB.9}
\]

and every `A_N x B_N` pair is one strict-rhombus crossing. The `A_N` rows are pairwise noncrossing because they belong to the selected O--O path. The `B_N` columns are pairwise noncrossing because two of them plus `tau_a` would otherwise form a triangle in the complete-train crossing graph.

At `N=8`,

\[
b_8=0.6536-{3.0605\over5}=0.0415>0,
\]

so every `N>=8` in the useful range starts with a genuine linear transverse shore.

---

## 2. The same full block synchronizes with an equally large diameter family

Because `J` is eligible, it is entirely on one hub side. Reverse the path if necessary and suppose `J` is pre-hub. Since `J` is not the first path block, the preceding block lies farther from the hub and contains

\[
>a_ND-o(D)
\]

path rows and therefore the same asymptotic number of internal pre-hub complementary regions available for births.

Choose `a_ND-o(D)` distinct internal regions strictly outward of the first row of `J`; the possible one-row block-size discrepancy is absorbed by the `o(D)` term. Phase-free region saturation supplies at least one actual switching diameter born in each. The one-sided switching theorem sends the other endpoint hubward, beyond every row of `J`. Hence each chosen diameter support contains every selected cut in `A_N`.

The bars are distinct because their birth regions are distinct and an actual diameter support containing the hub cannot have two distinct pre-hub birth regions.

Thus there is a family `E_N` with

\[
\boxed{|E_N|>a_ND-o(D)}
\tag{IB.10}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
E_N\times A_N
\text{ complete in exact selected-cut support incidence.}
}
\tag{IB.11}
\]

The post-hub case is identical after reversing the path.

This is the improvement over the previous hub/median construction: the whole train block survives. The synchronization density is `rho/N`, rather than one quarter of a preselected block density.

---

## 3. Metric span is automatic on every synchronized block

Apply the exact train-step metric lemma to the complete rectangle `A_N x B_N`. Every row has crossing-center span

\[
>{\sqrt3\over2}(|B_N|-1),
\]

and every column has crossing-center span

\[
>{\sqrt3\over2}(|A_N|-1)
\]

in its train-adapted monotone coordinate.

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rowspan}(A_N,B_N)
>
\left({\sqrt3\over2}b_N-o(1)\right)D,
}
\tag{IB.12}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{colspan}(A_N,B_N)
>
\left({\sqrt3\over2}a_N-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{IB.13}
\]

The selected O--O rows lie in one shrinking dominant phase window, so the row-adapted coordinates in (IB.12) can be replaced by one common row-normal coordinate with `o(D)` loss.

---

## 4. Diameter-heavy specialization: `N=8`

For `N=8`,

\[
a_8={0.0002038\over8}=2.5475\times10^{-5},
\]

\[
b_8=0.0415.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{|E_8|,|A_8|>2.5475\times10^{-5}D-o(D),}
\tag{IB.14}
\]

\[
\boxed{|B_8|>0.0415D-o(D).}
\tag{IB.15}
\]

Fixing one physical birth host among the `E_8` bars and using same-host diameter exclusion plus support ordering gives a strictly nested opposite-host-or-terminal rail of density

\[
\boxed{1.27375\times10^{-5}D-o(D).}
\tag{IB.16}
\]

Fixing one of those two physical destinations leaves

\[
\boxed{6.36875\times10^{-6}D-o(D)}
\tag{IB.17}
\]

strictly nested diameter bars, all spanning the same complete row block `A_8`.

This is the strongest current guaranteed cardinality for a diameter rail that is simultaneously locked to one complete linear transverse train family.

---

## 5. Product-optimal specialization: `N=14`

The raw synchronized grid product coefficient is

\[
f(N):=a_Nb_N
={\rho\over N}\left(r-{T\over N-3}\right).
\tag{IB.18}
\]

For real `x>3`, its derivative has sign opposite to

\[
6536x^2-100426x+150639.
\]

The larger root is

\[
13.680331\ldots,
\]

so the integer maximizer over `N>=8` is

\[
\boxed{N=14.}
\tag{IB.19}
\]

At `N=14`,

\[
\boxed{
a_{14}={1019\over70{,}000{,}000}
=1.4557142857\ldots\times10^{-5},
}
\tag{IB.20}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
b_{14}={41291\over110000}
=0.3753727272\ldots .
}
\tag{IB.21}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{|E_{14}|,|A_{14}|>1.45571428\times10^{-5}D-o(D),}
\tag{IB.22}
\]

\[
\boxed{|B_{14}|>0.3753727D-o(D).}
\tag{IB.23}
\]

The complete strict-rhombus product coefficient is

\[
\boxed{a_{14}b_{14}=5.4643544\ldots\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{IB.24}
\]

Every synchronized row has common dominant-phase metric span

\[
>(0.32508-o(1))D
\]

across the full `B_14` shore; the exact certificate retains the symbolic `sqrt(3)b_14/2` value rather than using this rounded display.

---

## 6. Re-purifying phase after the `N=14` synchronization

Apply the same orientation reduction as in the phase-collapse theorem. After deleting only `O(1)` ambiguous columns, one orientation class `B_{14,+}` has

\[
\boxed{|B_{14,+}|>{b_{14}\over2}D-o(D)
>0.1876863D-o(D).}
\tag{IB.25}
\]

The exact additive phase decomposition

\[
\delta_{ab}=g+r_a+q_b
\]

holds on `A_14 x B_{14,+}`. The global angular-defect sum is still less than

\[
C_{phase}D,
\qquad
C_{phase}={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\]

The product density before phase thinning is

\[
a_{14}{b_{14}\over2}
=2.7321772\ldots\times10^{-6}.
\tag{IB.26}
\]

Hence the average angular defect on this already synchronized product is at most

\[
{1.945888\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{IB.27}
\]

Using the post-synchronization Markov theorem with `epsilon=0.1`, retain ninety percent of each train shore. The entire diameter family `E_14` remains unthinned. Thus there are

\[
\boxed{|A_{14,90}|>1.31014285\times10^{-5}D-o(D),}
\tag{IB.28}
\]

\[
\boxed{|B_{14,90}|>0.1689177D-o(D),}
\tag{IB.29}
\]

while

\[
\boxed{|E_{14}|>1.45571428\times10^{-5}D-o(D).}
\tag{IB.30}
\]

Every `A_14,90 x B_14,90` crossing is in one common orientation and satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{\pi\over3}<\theta_{ab}
<{\pi\over3}+{4.09\times10^7\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{IB.31}
\]

The purified grid still has more than

\[
\boxed{(2.2130\times10^{-6}-o(1))D^2}
\tag{IB.32}
\]

strict-rhombus crossings.

Because both purified phase spreads are `O(1/D)`, the metric train coordinates transfer to two common directions with only `O(1)` loss. Every retained row has crossing-center span

\[
\boxed{>(0.14628-o(1))D,}
\tag{IB.33}
\]

and every retained column has span

\[
\boxed{>(1.1346\times10^{-5}-o(1))D.}
\tag{IB.34}
\]

This dominates the preceding ninety-percent post-synchronization scaffold in both train-shore sizes and has a smaller inverse-`D` angle constant.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The synchronized high-`t` geometry can now be chosen from a one-parameter family rather than from one fixed nine-block extraction.

```text
N=8  : maximize guaranteed synchronized diameter/row density
        E,A > 2.5475e-5 D, B > 0.0415 D

N=14 : maximize guaranteed complete-grid product
        E,A > 1.4557e-5 D, B > 0.37537 D
        after orientation + 90% phase purification:
        E > 1.4557e-5 D (unthinned)
        A > 1.3101e-5 D
        B > 0.168917 D
        all angles = pi/3 + O(1/D)
```

The previous `5.65e-6D` synchronized diameter count was not a structural limit; it came from synchronizing only after a particular rectangle block had already been chosen and then paying a side/median loss. Interior-block averaging eliminates that loss.

The remaining obstruction is unchanged in kind but now has much more room: exact support incidence still does not force a straight diameter chord to cross a row inside the train-grid patch. Any future channel/order argument should use the `N=8` diameter-heavy scaffold if it needs many nested diameter bars, or the `N=14` product-optimal purified scaffold if it needs the largest near-triangular train rectangle.

**Disposition:** replaced the fixed synchronized rectangle by an optimized interior-block family; increased the guaranteed synchronized actual-diameter population to `2.5475e-5D` in the diameter-heavy choice and produced a product-optimal `N=14` inverse-`D` grid with larger row and column shores than the previous post-synchronization phase scaffold. C-II and Level I remain open.
